#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116148} :activity/id #uuid "abceb987-e559-5c80-8a21-441171253b50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048419 :topic/id #uuid "9d572d01-c598-509f-894d-a4f2b6bee64e" :topic/math-academy-id 1584} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116503 :tutorial/content "In general, for $k>0$,\n\n- the graph of $y=f(x-k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *right*, while\n- the graph of $y=f(x+k)$ is a horizontal translation of $y=f(x)$ by $k$ units to the *left*.\n\n**Watch out!** It may seem counterintuitive that subtracting $k$ moves the graph to the right, while adding $k$ moves the graph to the left. However, this is in fact true.\n\nTo see why, let's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. Let's use this graph to plot the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\n![](images/6c/6ca96bba855899f9b87ae06c166503c9af739fcd6bb2ec466a73b75cc70dde7f.png)\n\nLet's take some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ and find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor example, take the point $(3,-4)$. The fact that it's on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means that\n\n$$\nf(3)=-4\n$$\n\nThen if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(x-2) = f({\\color{red}5}-2) = f(3) = -4\n$$\n\nwhich means that $({\\color{red}5},-4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ with the same $y$-value and a $x$-value that is $2$ units *greater*! Several more examples of points are shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x-2)[/math] \\\\ (3,-4) & (5,-4) \\\\ (1, 2) & (3, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (2, 1.5) \\\\ (-2, 0) & (0, 0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's plot both curves.\n\n![](images/ed/edae088984432dc7d0fff816a9e016ad221a3c897a78cf79efe6c784fa9176fe.png)\n\nWe see that every point on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$ is shifted $2$ units *to the right* of the corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "da231b95-be14-58c3-a8fe-6261d61c7d83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3280 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6345469f-1236-5eaf-8f5f-c51d47bf4f3c" :step/math-academy-id 18017 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115088 :question/id #uuid "e320a97a-d524-5225-895e-76034e599fa9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4973"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "372e7248-7fe0-5138-896d-61b4866f83cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050647}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115507}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Horizontal Translation of a Given Graph"} :step/id #uuid "d171b522-79a1-5dbc-bcf1-aa7179ecd080" :step/math-academy-id 18018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115089 :question/id #uuid "4dec57af-1c02-56fe-b38e-e62795fdc93d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "919da198-25e0-5b6f-8d99-79f1508759b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115622}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally Translated Function"} :step/id #uuid "b5c16920-e146-5ef0-841d-e2645a4e1de6" :step/math-academy-id 18019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116151}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116504 :tutorial/content "When graphing a function, we can combine vertical and horizontal translations. In general, the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x-h)+k\n$$\n\nis a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $h$ units to the right and $k$ units up.\n\nIt doesn't matter which translation we do first, but it's often easiest to start with the horizontal translation." :tutorial/id #uuid "ceb4fcff-ae84-5217-aa0c-16d64fc23eac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3282 :tutorial/title "Combining Vertical and Horizontal Translations"} :step/id #uuid "c6cc0af1-c408-599c-ac0a-69b7af985fac" :step/math-academy-id 18020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115090 :question/id #uuid "86b96055-a0e1-5dc7-9f89-364f77412a77" :question/math-academy-id "e-4975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c84dd5-2c76-5584-81a1-53d2459afb94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233127}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting Horizontal and Vertical Translations of a Given Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b18fd01e-e160-5b17-a9cf-730b2b261357" :step/math-academy-id 18022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115103 :question/id #uuid "740d5deb-3982-5293-9071-ddaaa189fb81" :question/math-academy-id "e-5229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57ef1e2c-07d4-5d04-9de8-8884cae17c00" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115621}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Corresponding Point on a Horizontally and Vertically Translated Function"} :step/id #uuid "5cdd1f56-3cc3-5455-896a-aec5448b7269" :step/math-academy-id 19261}] :activity/title "Horizontal Translations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115418}]}
